^Welcome to the Lingonaut Finnish course! We are happy that you are here.^


The Finnish language belongs to the *Finno-Ugric* language group along with Estonian, Hungarian and a few others. This means Finnish is not related to any of the Indo-European languages like English, German or French, but has still obtained a few loanwords from Swedish and Russian for example. What this also means is that it is good to begin learning the Finnish language with an open mind, because some things work very differently than what you might be used to. For example, the sentence 'I will go to school with my car' is just two words in Finnish, *menen kouluun autollani* ('go-I school-into car-with-my'). What makes stuff easier is that there are no articles (*talo* can mean both 'a house' and 'the house') and there are no genders for nouns. Additionally, *prepositions* (small words like 'in', 'from', 'on' in English), that are spearate words in other languages, are in Finnish instead added to the end of the noun, and are called *suffixes*. For example, the sentence 'in your house' translated to Finnish becomes *talossasi* ('house-inside-your') and even more suffixes can be added onto a word depending on what you want to say.

^About this course^
This course begins by teaching you some simple words, greetings and goodbyes, as well as pronouns and the verb 'to be' (saying '_I am, you are, we are_'). This course will alternate between teaching you vocabulary and grammar, but there are certain things that you will learn best by repetition and memorizing key phrases. The skill notes will provide you with additional information, grammar explanations, tables etc. to help you understand why things are the way they are, as well as how to remember them. Feel free to screenshot them if you want to have easier access to them.


^Useful to know before beginning this course:^
- Finnish always puts the *emphasis on the first syllable* of a word, e.g. *HELsinki*, *SUOmi*, *TElevisio*
- every skill is explained in detail beforehand, you will benefit greatly if you take the time to read through the Skill Notes before diving in
- word endings, or *suffixes*, usually have multiple different forms and are written out with a dash like this: '-sta/-stä', '-aa/-ää' etc. [*-sta* and *-stä* are the same suffix, but the vowel has changed to correspond with the vowels of the word it is attached to]

^Sounds in Finnish^
One of the easy things with finnish is that it is pronounced quite exactly how it is written, one letter equals one sounds. Nevertheless, this can still present a challenge if you are not accustomed to an orthography like this. The table below tries to teach you how to pronounce the different sounds _like an English speaker would need to learn them_, but whatever your mother tongue is, it is recommended to practice this pronounciation a lot.

^Vowels^
*Note:* Vowels are pronounced short if there is only one, and long if there are two after each other. There are no exceptions to this rule. Compare *satu* (story) and *saatu* (gotten).


a = like ah in 'farther'     /ɑ/      pronounced in the back of the mouth
e = like in ‘pet’     /e/
i = like in ‘pit’     /i/
o = like in ‘awe’ or ‘naughty’     /o/
u = like ‘a’ but with rounded lips     /u/
y = ‘i’ like in ‘pin’ except with rounded lips     /y/      in Finnish this is a vowel not a consonant
ä = like ‘a’ in ‘man’     /æ/
ö = like ‘ä’ but with rounded lips     /ø/


*Note:* ‘ä’ and ‘ö’ might seem like just ‘a’ and ‘o’ with dots on top, but they are entirely separate letters and should be treated as such.
å = the same as the above ‘o’. This vowel actually doesn’t exist in the Finnish language and is only used when writing names and place names of Swedish origin that contain ‘å’, but is nevertheless good to know because it remains part of the Finnish alphabet.


Additionally, a big part of Finnish is *diphtongs*, i.e. two vowels next to each other, like L*au*ra, *ie*n, *yö* etc. Diphtongs are pronounced much more 'exaggerated' than in English, so don’t worry about overpronouncing them; it actually makes you sound more native and will make you more understood. If your native language is German or a similar one where two vowels combined can make a completely new set of sounds, like '*ei*-->*"ai"*', it is recommended to note that all letters in a Finnish word need to be pronounced like separate sounds. Similarly, for a German speaker it can also be hard to pronounce the middle 'h' in words like *huhta* (not pronounced [huuta]), but in fact both 'h' are very much pronounced with equal strength.

^Pronouncing consonants^
Consonants are mostly the same as in English. However, consonants are also pronounced long when there are two of them together, like with vowels. Compare *pallo* (ball) and *palo* (fire), or *pyssy* (gun) and *pysy!* (hold!). This can be a tricky thing to learn with consonants that cannot be stretched, like 't'. In the word *matto* (carpet) the t-sound is made longer by 'waiting' or 'closing the sound box' between 'a' and 'tt', pronouncing it [ma.to]. In other words, you make your tongue ready to make the t-sound but you wait a second before actually pronouncing it.
This might sound awkward or clumsy for a beginner, but this is in fact normal speech and it will make you sound much more natural. There are a load of words in Finnish that have a completely different meaning if you mispronounce the length of a vowel or underpronounce a double consonant, especially with short words. This is also one of the reasons some people say Finnish sounds a little like Japanese.
Although the alphabet is mostly the same as in English, the sounds ‘b’, ‘f’ and ‘g’ are not native to Finnish. They do exist in newer words but are complicated for a native to pronounce.


J = 'y' in 'yay'
R = rolled, which can be challenging in the beginning
W = V
Z = 'ts'


Some consonants together are pronounced differently:


nk = 'ngk' in 'Ki*ngk*ong'
np = becomes 'mp' in flowing speech
uv = like English 'w'

^This is a lot to take in but you will have much time to learn this during the course. For now, the most important thing is that you have at least seen this. You can now begin the course. Good luck!^